# OwnProps and PassThroughProps

When a React wrapper forwards most of a child component's props, separate the
wrapper-owned inputs from the forwarded child surface:

```tsx
interface OwnProps {
  redaction: GetRedactionResponse;
}

interface PassThroughProps extends Omit<
  RedactionBoundingBoxListProps,
  'redactionBoundingBoxes'
> {}

export interface RedactionPanelProps extends OwnProps, PassThroughProps {}
```

Destructure the owned props and forward the remainder. Put wrapper-generated
values after the spread so they override pass-through values:

```tsx
const { redaction, ...passThroughProps } = props;

// Ensures that we don't pass through more props than needed
const _ownProps: RequiredWithUndefined<OwnProps> = { redaction };

return (
  <RedactionBoundingBoxList
    {...passThroughProps}
    redactionBoundingBoxes={redaction.redactionBoundingBoxes}
  />
);
```

- Always add `const _ownProps: RequiredWithUndefined<OwnProps>` after
  destructuring. TypeScript then fails if a new owned field is forgotten and
  would otherwise leak into `passThroughProps`.
- Use `Omit` when nearly all child props pass through; use `Pick` for a small,
  deliberate child surface.
- Keep `OwnProps` and `PassThroughProps` private unless another module needs
  them.
- Keep the child prop type as the source of truth so adding a child prop does
  not require repeating it in the wrapper interface.
